  <keyword>edf.merge</keyword>
  <short_description>Concatenate compatible EDF or BDF files end to end in time.</short_description>
  <syntax>
    <syntax_item>edf.merge(dst, sources)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>edf.merge(dst, sources, 'RecordSeconds', s)</syntax_item>
    <syntax_item>info = edf.merge(...)</syntax_item>
  </syntax>
  <param_input>
    <param_input_item>
      <param_name>dst</param_name>
      <param_description>the destination file.</param_description>
    </param_input_item>
    <param_input_item>
      <param_name>sources</param_name>
      <param_description>a cellstr of paths, read in order; every file must carry the same signals (labels and rates) as the first.</param_description>
    </param_input_item>
    <param_input_item>
      <param_name>s</param_name>
      <param_description>the output record length in seconds; the default is the first file's.</param_description>
    </param_input_item>
  </param_input>
  <param_output>
    <param_output_item>
      <param_name>info</param_name>
      <param_description>the edf.write info of the file written.</param_description>
    </param_output_item>
  </param_output>
  <description>
    <p>The signals are concatenated into one continuous recording; the annotations of each file are shifted by the time already written, so a mark keeps its place on the merged timeline. The physical range is recomputed over the merged data, so a value outside any one file's range is not clamped.</p>
  </description>
  <used_function />
  <bibliography />
  <examples>
    <example_item>
      <example_item_type>nelson</example_item_type>
      <example_item_description />
      <example_item_data><![CDATA[edf.merge('night.edf', {'part1.edf', 'part2.edf', 'part3.edf'});]]></example_item_data>
    </example_item>
  </examples>
